{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011Cfbm1ByBSB5p6xdf7a6LZ", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "signature": ""}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7414, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16780, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7414}, "output_tokens": 4, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "57544e78-049c-4499-b780-5317fff7bffe", "uuid": "169741ef-4f8d-4bb8-b554-563d6479b74a", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T15:32:41.111Z", "request_id": "req_011Cfbm1BeLk7fysHLFcDUAm"}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011Cfbm1ByBSB5p6xdf7a6LZ", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "**Banker's rounding** (also called round-half-to-even or convergent rounding) is a rule for ties, meaning values exactly halfway between two rounding targets. A tie goes to whichever neighbor is **even**:\n\n| Value | Round-half-up | Banker's (half-even) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0.5 | 1 | **0** |\n| 1.5 | 2 | **2** |\n| 2.5 | 3 | **2** |\n| 3.5 | 4 | **4** |\n| 2.675 \u2192 2 dp | 2.68 | **2.68** (7 is odd, so it goes up to 8) |\n| 2.665 \u2192 2 dp | 2.67 | **2.66** |\n\nValues that aren't exact ties round normally: 2.51 still becomes 3.\n\n### Why accountants like it\n\n1. **It removes upward bias.** With round-half-up, every tie goes up. Over thousands of line items (invoices, interest accruals, tax lines), totals drift upward by a small but steady amount. Half-even sends about half the ties up and half down, so the errors tend to cancel and rounded totals stay close to the true total.\n\n2. **Sums reconcile better.** When you round each line and then add them up, the result matches the rounded grand total more often. That means fewer one-cent differences between a ledger and its subledger, or between an invoice total and its lines.\n\n3. **It's a recognized standard.** IEEE 754 uses it as the default rounding mode for floating point. Many financial and statistical conventions use it too, and some regulators and tax authorities specify or accept it. It's easy to defend in an audit because it's documented and neutral.\n\n4. **It's fair across many transactions.** Neither side systematically gains the extra half-cent, which matters when you're rounding interest or fees across millions of accounts.\n\n### Caveats\n\n- **It only matters for exact ties.** In code, use a decimal type such as Python's `decimal.Decimal`, not binary floats. Many decimal values like 2.675 can't be stored exactly as floats, so the \"tie\" isn't really a tie and the result can look wrong.\n- **Rules vary by jurisdiction.** Some tax rules explicitly require round-half-up, so check what applies to you.\n- **Don't round in the middle of a calculation.** Rounding at intermediate steps compounds error no matter which mode you use. Round at the defined reporting points.\n\nFor this project specifically, a stored decision says tax amounts here use `ROUND_HALF_EVEN`, agreed with the finance team in March 2026."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7414, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16780, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7414}, "output_tokens": 4, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "57544e78-049c-4499-b780-5317fff7bffe", "uuid": "2cc1df56-86ff-474b-bd1e-ad1686720945", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T15:32:50.002Z", "request_id": "req_011Cfbm1BeLk7fysHLFcDUAm"}
